*I'm building [Caffeine](https://github.com/Brickell-Research/caffeine_lang), a compiler written in Gleam that compiles service expectations to reliability
artifacts. This post documents our migration from Deno to Bun and the tail call optimization rabbit hole that led us there.*

***

# We Build with Deno

In my talk at [Gleam Gathering](https://gleamgathering.com/), I have a slide where I discuss how we packaged up Caffeine:

{.figure}
![Packaging Caffeine slide from Gleam Gathering](/images/load-test-caffeine_packaging_caffeine_slide.png)

Slide 26 from my Gleam Gathering talk, "Packaging Caffeine"

[Most of the details can be found in this blog post](/blog/packaging-caffeine) if you're curious. TLDR is I couldn't quite get the _nix_ setup to work
so I ended up compiling to Javascript and then leveraging [Deno](https://deno.com/) to package it with a JS runtime.
[Some more details on this from their website](https://deno.com/blog/deno-compile-executable-programs).

## It's Large

One of the _downsides_ of this build setup is the size. Somewhat embarrasingly, I looked at the zipped/tarballed binaries and thought
"eh 40mb not too bad right?":

{.figure}
![Release sizes on GitHub](/images/load-test-caffeine_caffeine_release_sizes.png)

Recent `4.5.0` release of Caffeine on Github

However, that's compressed. Here is the reality:

```bash
➜ brew info caffeine_lang
==> brickell-research/caffeine/caffeine_lang: stable 4.5.1
Caffeine programming language
https://caffeine-lang.run
Installed
/opt/homebrew/Cellar/caffeine_lang/4.5.1 (4 files, 121MB) *
```

Ooof `121mb`. Not small. While relatively speaking that isn't the _most offensively sized language toolchain_, the actual
Caffeine compiler, CLI, and LSP together are only a small portion of the ~121mb. The rest is the Deno runtime
we're bundling along for the ride.

## Does It Actually Matter?

Well, for what it's worth this is a developer tool which will likely be limited to running on developer laptops (_which are typically fairly beefy_)
or within CICD pipelines. So the `121mb` isn't a barrier to entry per se.

The more interesting question is speed and scale: within typical use cases, how fast will Caffeine compile and at what point does compilation become
a noticeable bottleneck?

***

# Setting Up Benchmarks

To begin answering these questions, we set up [perf](https://github.com/Brickell-Research/perf):
_various benchmarks and corpuses and such for caffeine._

The basic idea:

1. Generate a bunch of corpuses of Caffeine blueprints and expectations of varying sizes

2. Script the benchmarking of these corpuses using [hyperfine](https://github.com/sharkdp/hyperfine)

3. Setup CICD such that we continuously run these benchmarks in a controlled fashion

Ideally this process would be fairly deterministic. We want to control: (a) hardware (_running within Github Actions_), (b) corpus
(_consistent between runs_), (c) warm-up (_need to understand the JS runtime_), and (d) build (_should use actual Caffeine releases_).

However, before going down the determinism rabbit hole (_been down there before, in 2019 I did an internship with the
[Stellar Development Foundation](https://stellar.org/) where I spent a bunch of time improving the determinism score in attempt to do some fuzz
testing with [AFL](https://afl-1.readthedocs.io/)_), let's just get this all setup first.

To bootstrap, I heavily leaned on Claude. I had it put together a corpus generator, wire up hyperfine, and build a script to compare against a baseline.
For starters, I decided on the following corpus sizes:

```
┌────────────────┬────────────┬──────────────┐
│     Scale      │ Blueprints │ Expectations │
├────────────────┼────────────┼──────────────┤
│ small          │ 2          │ 4            │
├────────────────┼────────────┼──────────────┤
│ medium         │ 5          │ 24           │
├────────────────┼────────────┼──────────────┤
│ large          │ 20         │ 120          │
├────────────────┼────────────┼──────────────┤
│ huge           │ 50         │ 600          │
├────────────────┼────────────┼──────────────┤
│ insane         │ 50         │ 6,000        │
├────────────────┼────────────┼──────────────┤
│ absurd         │ 50         │ 25,000       │
└────────────────┴────────────┴──────────────┘
```

Plus a set of single-blueprint scaling tests from 10 to 5,000 expectations to isolate how compilation time grows with expectation count.

## And Then It Blew Up

Upon generation of these files, I wanted to format them: `caffeine format .`

```bash
➜  perf git:(main) ✗ caffeine format .
error: Uncaught (in promise) RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded
import * as $float from "../../../gleam_stdlib/gleam/float.mjs";
^
    at tokenize_loop (file:///var/folders/.../tokenizer.mjs:1:1)
    at emit_token_n (file:///var/folders/.../tokenizer.mjs:1:1)
    at emit_token (file:///var/folders/.../tokenizer.mjs:1:1)
    at tokenize_loop (file:///var/folders/.../tokenizer.mjs:1:1)
    at emit_token_n (file:///var/folders/.../tokenizer.mjs:1:1)
    at emit_token (file:///var/folders/.../tokenizer.mjs:1:1)
    ...
```

Not ideal (_and this is before any actual benchmarking_). The stack trace tells the whole story if you squint:
`tokenize_loop → emit_token → emit_token_n → tokenize_loop → emit_token → ...`. We had an infinite-looking chain of mutual
recursion that ate through the entire call stack.

***

# Tail Call Optimization

Ok, so what's actually going on here?

## The Short Version

Within the functional programming paradigm, it's quite common for functions to call themselves recursively as the last step if some earlier base
case isn't met. When a recursive call is the _very last thing_ a function does (_the "tail position"_), a smart runtime can avoid pushing a new
stack frame entirely, it just jumps back to the top of the function. This is called [*tail call optimization*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail_call) (TCO).

Consider a tail-recursive loop in Gleam:

```gleam
fn sum_loop(list: List(Int), acc: Int) -> Int {
  case list {
    [] -> acc
    [first, ..rest] -> sum_loop(rest, acc + first)
  }
}
```

The recursive call to `sum_loop` is the _very last thing_ that happens. There's no work left after it returns. That's what makes it a tail
call. Gleam compiles to both Erlang and Javascript. On Erlang's BEAM VM, tail calls are [optimized natively](https://learnyousomeerlang.com/recursion) as the BEAM was designed for this.
But on Javascript, it depends on the engine.

*V8 doesn't implement tail call optimization. And there's no indication it ever will.*

V8 had it behind the `--harmony-tailcalls` flag around 2016, then removed it. The team [cited debugging concerns](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-ptc-syntax)
(tail-called frames disappear from stack traces) and chose developer experience over spec compliance. SpiderMonkey
(_Firefox_) never implemented it either. Thus, even though the [ES6 spec](https://262.ecma-international.org/6.0/#sec-tail-position-calls)
mandates proper tail calls, but V8 and SpiderMonkey simply ignore that part of the spec.

Deno uses V8 and so Deno doesn't have TCO.

## What Gleam Does About It

The smart folks on the Gleam core team have partially solved this with a loop transformation. When the Gleam JS backend detects a function that
calls itself in the tail position, it compiles it to a `while(true)` loop instead of actual recursion, reassigning the arguments
rather than making a new call. With this we get constant stack space. Problem solved!

Except not always...

## How `result.try` Breaks It

Gleam's `use` syntax is sugar for passing a callback. This:

```gleam
use #(item, state) <- result.try(parse_something(state))
parse_items_loop(state, [item, ..acc])
```

Desugars to:

```gleam
result.try(parse_something(state), fn(tuple) {
  let #(item, state) = tuple
  parse_items_loop(state, [item, ..acc])
})
```

Which compiles to roughly this JS:

```javascript
function parse_items_loop(state, acc) {
  return result_try(
    parse_something(state),
    (tuple) => {
      let [item, state2] = tuple;
      return parse_items_loop(state2, [item, ...acc]);
    }
  );
}

function result_try(result, callback) {
  if (result.isOk()) {
    return callback(result.value);
  }
  return result;
}
```

The recursive `parse_items_loop` call is in tail position _of the closure_, but not of `parse_items_loop` itself. From `parse_items_loop`'s
perspective, the last thing it does is call `result_try(...)`. And from `result_try`'s perspective, the last thing it does is call
`callback(...)`. Neither of those is a self-call, so neither gets optimized into a loop.

Every iteration adds two stack frames: one for `parse_items_loop` and one for `result_try`. With 5,000 expectations, that's 10,000+ frames.
V8's stack holds roughly 10,000-15,000 frames.

## Same Story for `bool.guard`

Same pattern, different function. `use <- bool.guard(when: condition, return: Error(...))` wraps the recursive call in a thunk
passed to `bool_guard`. The indirection hides the tail call from the compiler, an identical problem to `result.try`.

## And Mutual Recursion

This one is different. Our tokenizer had a chain across three separate functions:

```
tokenize_loop → emit_token → emit_token_n → tokenize_loop
```

`tokenize_loop` matches a character, calls `emit_token` to record it, which calls `emit_token_n` to advance the position,
which calls back to `tokenize_loop` to process the next character. The final call _is_ in tail position of `emit_token_n`,
and `emit_token_n` _is_ in tail position of `emit_token`, and so on up the chain. But Gleam's JS backend only optimizes
_self_-tail-calls: function A calling function A. It can't see that this chain of three functions is effectively a loop.
This is a [known open issue](https://github.com/gleam-lang/gleam/issues/3830).

***

# The Unwrapping

So we unwrapped everything. We replaced higher-order functions with direct `case` expressions so the recursive call lands in tail position of
the function itself.

For `result.try`:

```gleam
// Before: closure hides the tail call
use #(item, state) <- result.try(parse_something(state))
parse_items_loop(state, [item, ..acc])

// After: direct case, recursive call in tail position
case parse_something(state) {
  Ok(#(item, state)) -> parse_items_loop(state, [item, ..acc])
  Error(e) -> Error(e)
}
```

The compiled JS goes from nested closures to:

```javascript
function parse_items_loop(state, acc) {
  while (true) {
    const result = parse_something(state);
    if (result.isOk()) {
      let [item, state2] = result.value;
      acc = [item, ...acc];
      state = state2;
      // falls through to next iteration — no stack growth
    }
    return new Error(result.error);
  }
}
```

For `bool.guard`, same idea: replace the thunk with a plain `case` so the recursive call is direct.

For the mutual recursion in the tokenizer, we inlined `emit_token` and `emit_token_n` directly into `tokenize_loop`,
collapsing the three-function chain into a single self-recursive function. One function calling itself. Optimizable.

All three cases are the same fundamental issue: *an intermediary sits between the recursive call and the function that needs to loop*.
Unwrapping removes the intermediary so the recursive call is a direct self-tail-call.

This worked! The compiler stopped crashing. But we were fighting the engine *and more importantly, fighting the language*.

***

# Enter the Fine Folks Working on Safari

[JavaScriptCore](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/javascriptcore), the engine behind Safari and *Bun*, is the only major
JS engine that implements proper tail calls per the ES6 spec, and has since 2016. They solved the debugging problem with
[ShadowChicken](https://webkit.org/blog/6240/ecmascript-6-proper-tail-calls-in-webkit/), a shadow stack that reconstructs
eliminated frames in the inspector.

The key difference: V8 doesn't optimize _any_ tail-position calls. JavaScriptCore optimizes _all_ of them, not just
self-calls but any call in tail position of any function. So that `result_try` → `callback` → `parse_items_loop` chain...
every call in that chain is in tail position of its caller. JavaScriptCore sees that and eliminates the frames. V8 just stacks them up
until it overflows.

*ASIDE*: we're not anti-Deno! It's a great runtime with a strong security model and excellent tooling. But for our specific case
(_a deeply recursive compiler written in a functional language_), V8's lack of TCO was a fundamental constraint.
So we switched to [Bun](https://bun.sh/).

## Bun Migration 

The migration was small:

1. Remove `npm:` import prefixes from a handful of TypeScript files

2. Swap `Deno.args` for `process.argv.slice(2)`

3. Change the build command

The compiled Gleam output is pure ESM that runs on any runtime without changes. We then undid all the manual unwrapping, restored `result.try`,
`bool.guard`, and the natural mutual recursion in the tokenizer. The code is cleaner. It is idiomatic Gleam again. And it's faster!

### Benchmarking

Here's `v4.5.1` (_Deno, with all the manual TCO workarounds_) vs `v4.6.0` (_Bun, with natural recursive style restored_):

#### Complexity Benchmarks

```
┌──────────────────────────┬────────────────┬──────────────┬─────────┐
│ Corpus                   │ Deno (v4.5.1)  │ Bun (v4.6.0) │ Speedup │
├──────────────────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ small (2 bp, 4 exp)      │       71.9 ms  │     60.7 ms  │   1.18x │
├──────────────────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ medium (5 bp, 24 exp)    │      149.2 ms  │    114.8 ms  │   1.30x │
├──────────────────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ large (20 bp, 120 exp)   │      625.5 ms  │    405.8 ms  │   1.54x │
├──────────────────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ huge (50 bp, 600 exp)    │         5.0 s  │       2.5 s  │   2.00x │
├──────────────────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ insane (50 bp, ~6K exp)  │         8.1 s  │       4.2 s  │   1.92x │
├──────────────────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ absurd (50 bp, ~25K exp) │        26.3 s  │      13.5 s  │   1.95x │
└──────────────────────────┴────────────────┴──────────────┴─────────┘
```

#### Scaling Benchmarks (single blueprint)

```
┌──────────────┬────────────────┬──────────────┬─────────┐
│ Expectations │ Deno (v4.5.1)  │ Bun (v4.6.0) │ Speedup │
├──────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ 10           │      212.9 ms  │    146.5 ms  │   1.45x │
├──────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ 50           │      354.7 ms  │    246.6 ms  │   1.44x │
├──────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ 100          │      566.5 ms  │    376.1 ms  │   1.51x │
├──────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ 500          │         2.1 s  │       1.4 s  │   1.55x │
├──────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ 1,000        │         4.0 s  │       2.6 s  │   1.56x │
├──────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ 2,500        │        10.6 s  │       6.6 s  │   1.60x │
├──────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────┤
│ 5,000        │        26.0 s  │      13.6 s  │   1.92x │
└──────────────┴────────────────┴──────────────┴─────────┘
```

Here you can see the Bun (plus proper recursive design) version is anywhere from *1.2x to 2x faster* depending on corpus size. The speedup grows as the corpus gets larger, which makes
sense because more expectations means deeper recursion and is where native TCO really shines.

*ASIDE*: a caveat this isn't a pure apples-to-apples runtime comparison. v4.5.1 had the manual TCO workarounds in the code while
v4.6.0 restored natural recursive style. The speedup reflects the combined effect of switching runtimes _and_ writing
cleaner code. We haven't isolated how much comes from JavaScriptCore's raw speed vs. native TCO vs. the code changes themselves.

#### Binary Size

```bash
➜ brew info caffeine_lang
==> brickell-research/caffeine/caffeine_lang: stable 4.6.0
Caffeine programming language
https://caffeine-lang.run
Installed
/opt/homebrew/Cellar/caffeine_lang/4.6.0 (4 files, 62.1MB) *
```

Down from `121mb` to `62mb`. Nearly half. Bun's runtime is also significantly smaller than Deno's.

***

# Conclusion

While we came to define a benchmark, we immediately hit a stack overflow, and following the TCO rabbit hole ended up switching runtimes. What a ride!

Now with the benchmarking pipeline in place, we're set up to track performance as Caffeine evolves. Next up is getting these
benchmarks running deterministically in GitHub Actions so we can catch regressions before they ship!
